      <h1>Do You Need to Offer Buyer’s Compensation?</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A practical look at what has changed with buyer’s agent compensation and how sellers in Grapevine, Flower Mound, Colleyville, and Southlake can think through the decision.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2>What has changed</h2>
      <p>
        For many years, most listing agreements included an offer of compensation to the buyer’s agent. That amount was often published in the MLS and became a standard part of how homes were marketed. Recent industry changes mean sellers are no longer required to offer compensation to a buyer’s agent through the MLS in the same automatic way.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sellers can still choose to offer compensation if they believe it will help attract more buyers or make their home more competitive. The difference is that the decision is now more explicit. It is something to discuss at the beginning of the listing rather than something that happens by default.
      </p>

      <h2>Why some sellers still choose to offer it</h2>
      <p>
        Offering compensation to a buyer’s agent can reduce friction for potential buyers. Some buyers have limited cash for closing costs and prefer working with an agent who will be compensated through the transaction. When a seller offers compensation, it can make the home accessible to a wider pool of buyers without requiring those buyers to pay their agent out of pocket.
      </p>
      <p>
        In a market with multiple homes available, some sellers decide that offering compensation is a reasonable way to keep their listing competitive. Others prefer to negotiate it on a case-by-case basis once an offer arrives. Both approaches are valid depending on the property and the seller’s goals.
      </p>

      <h2>When sellers may choose not to offer it</h2>
      <p>
        Some sellers prefer not to include an offer of compensation up front. They may want more flexibility to evaluate each offer individually, or they may be working with buyers who are prepared to pay their own agent. In stronger markets or with highly desirable homes, some sellers have found that buyers are still willing to move forward even when compensation is not pre-offered.
      </p>
      <p>
        The trade-off is that some buyer’s agents may be less inclined to show the home, or some buyers may need to structure their offer differently. This does not mean the home will not sell. It simply means the process can involve more direct conversation between the parties about how the buyer’s representation will be handled.
      </p>

      <h2>How to think through the decision</h2>
      <p>
        There is no single correct answer for every home. The practical questions are usually:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>How competitive is the current market for homes like yours?</li>
        <li>Do you want to attract the broadest possible pool of buyers, including those who prefer their agent to be compensated through the sale?</li>
        <li>Would you rather decide on a case-by-case basis once offers come in?</li>
        <li>How important is speed versus maximum flexibility in the negotiation?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Discussing these points early with your listing agent helps set clear expectations. Most sellers care more about understanding the options and the likely trade-offs than about following any single industry norm.
      </p>

      <h2>A practical approach</h2>
      <p>
        At Shaver Realty Group we walk through this decision with each seller based on the home and the current market. Some sellers choose to offer compensation as part of the listing strategy. Others prefer to leave it open and address it when an offer is received. The goal is to make the choice intentional rather than automatic.
      </p>
      <p>
        Clear communication on this point, along with transparent listing fees, tends to reduce confusion later in the process. Sellers who understand both the listing fee and the buyer compensation question up front are usually better prepared for the offers that arrive.
      </p>

